EXT. FERRY LANDING - MISSOURI RIVER - NOON

A flat-bottomed ferry tied to a splintered dock. Mud, cottonwoods, a warped sign: CROSSING 5 CENTS. Heat shimmers off the water.

ISAAC DELAROSA, fifties, ferryman, sits on a barrel whittling a peg. TOBIAS "TOBY" WEATHERBY, twenties, a cattle driver, leans on a hitching post and fans himself with his hat. Behind them, MRS. ETHELINDA PRIEST, sixty, in black bombazine, waits with a hatbox on her knee. A silent drifter, HOLLOWAY STROUD, forties, sits on the edge of the dock with his boots hanging over the water.

TOBY
(to the group)
Anyone else think this river's been getting wider?

ISAAC
It's the same river.

TOBY
I swear it's wider.

MRS. PRIEST
(to Isaac)
Sir, when does the ferry depart?

ISAAC
When it's loaded.

MRS. PRIEST
It is loaded.

ISAAC
It has one passenger and a hatbox.

MRS. PRIEST
Then I shall speak with the other passengers.

She turns to Toby and Holloway. Toby grins, puts on his hat. Holloway doesn't look up.

HOLLOWAY
(muttering)
Won't be long now.

Isaac sets down his knife. Toby's grin fades. Mrs. Priest tilts her head.

MRS. PRIEST
Did you say something, sir?

HOLLOWAY
Talking to the river, ma'am.

The sound of hooves comes from the bluff. Three riders appear on the ridge, silhouetted against the sun. Toby straightens. Isaac stands. Holloway rises slowly, brushing off his knees.

TOBY
(to the group)
Folks, I believe we have company.

ISAAC
Mrs. Priest, get behind the barrel.

MRS. PRIEST
I will do nothing of the sort.

HOLLOWAY
Ma'am.

She looks at him. He opens his coat. Under it, a deputy's badge.

HOLLOWAY (CONT'D)
That'll be Ned Cutler and his boys. I've been waiting on them since Tuesday.

Isaac and Toby stare at him. Mrs. Priest sets the hatbox on the dock, opens it, and lifts out a short-barreled revolver.

MRS. PRIEST
(coolly)
I, too, have been waiting.

The riders start down the bluff. Toby dives behind the post, Isaac drops behind the barrel, Holloway draws his pistol, and Mrs. Priest, with perfect composure, cocks the hammer and steps onto the open dock.
